# Special events, exceptions and interrupts.

@0D114 process_special_event
    ===========================================================================
    Special event processing
    ===========================================================================
    Every emulation routine ends with DISPATCH, which falls through to here
    instead of entering the next instruction when cr_special_event_pending is
    set.  At this point pc and prefetch_data already describe the next
    instruction and disp_table addresses its dispatch table entry.

    The events, in the order they are tested:
        odd pc              -> address error exception
        trace pending       -> trace exception
        interrupt pending   -> interrupt exception, if its level beats the mask
        test-mode flags     -> debugging / profiling hooks
        SR T1/T0 set        -> arrange a trace exception after the next instruction

    The first thing done is an indirect call through a hook in the emulator
    state, which normally points straight back to special_event_continue.
0D114 hook; normally special_event_continue
0D118 flag_odd_pc = low bit of pc
0D120 cr4..cr7 = flag byte and SR byte of sr_and_flags
0D124 pending = "other event" flag (re-evaluated below)
0D128 CTR = dispatch entry of the next instruction

@0D130 special_event_continue
0D130 odd pc
0D134 trace pending from the previous instruction
0D138 trace needed if T1 or T0
0D13C look for a pending interrupt
0D148 nothing left to do: run the instruction

@0D14C arm_trace
    The next instruction is to be traced.  Remember where it starts and keep
    the special event pending so that control comes back here once it has
    run.  With T1 (trace all) that is unconditional; with T0 (trace on change
    of flow) the opcode is examined and trace_pending is only left set for
    instructions that change the flow of control.
0D150 pc is 2 past the instruction
0D158 flag_trace_pending
0D15C T1: trace every instruction
0D160 data = the opcode about to run

@0D228 take_trace_exception
0D238 format 2, vector offset 0x24 (trace)
0D240 format 2 carries the address of the traced instruction

@0D248 take_interrupt
    Take the pending interrupt if its level is above the SR mask (level 7 is
    not maskable).  Uses the autovector for the level, counts the interrupt,
    and if the master stack is active builds the 68020 "throwaway" frame on
    the interrupt stack as well.
0D268 0xC0 + 4*level: per-level interrupt counter in the state block
0D274 0x60 + 4*level: autovector offset
0D288 SR interrupt mask = level
0D290 push SR
0D294 push PC
0D298 push format/vector word
0D2A0 clear SR M bit
0D2A8 format 1 (throwaway) for the second frame

@0D2B4 test_mode_events
    The upper half of sr_and_flags holds debugging switches.  Each one that
    is set calls a small routine before the instruction runs.
0D2BC bit 0x2000_0000: consistency checks
0D2C0 bit 0x0001_0000: count instructions
0D2C4 bit 0x0002_0000: count instructions per opcode
0D2D0 bit 0x0004_0000: call a hook

@0D2F8 check_prefetch_consistency
    Debugging aid: trap if prefetch_data, CTR or disp_table do not agree with
    the instruction stream at pc.
@0D31C count_instruction
@0D32C count_opcode

@0D344 check_double_fault
    Trap to the kernel if a group 1 exception (bus or address error) happens
    while another is still being processed.
@0D34C take_address_error
    Build a 68020 format $B (long bus fault) frame for an address error.
0D34C clear the odd-pc marker in disp_table
0D350 flag_group_1_active
0D358 format $B, vector offset 0x00C (address error)
0D36C the frame is 92 bytes in all

@0D3E4 check_interrupt
    Called from special event processing.  Returns unless an interrupt above
    the current mask is requested; otherwise marks it pending.

@0D440 native_exception_handler
    Entered by the kernel when the processor takes an exception while the
    emulator context is running (its address is in ecb_handler).  On entry
    data (r4) holds ecb_handler_arg, the address of take_bus_error, and LR
    holds ecb_handler_return, the address of EmKernelTrapTable; the kernel
    has stored its description of the fault in the context block.
    .
    Chooses where the emulator should resume - take_bus_error, or
    take_address_error if the fault was the deliberate jump past the
    dispatch table for an odd pc - stores that in ecb_pc, copies the fault
    details to where take_bus_error expects them, and returns.  Returning
    lands on slot 0 of the trap table, ReturnFromException, with r3 = 0.
0D474 ecb_pc = where to resume

@0D4C8 take_bus_error
    Build a 68020 format $B (long bus fault) frame for a bus error.
0D4E0 format $B, vector offset 0x008 (bus error)

@0D750 take_illegal_instruction
    Dispatch table entries for unimplemented opcodes load a handler address
    from the state block into rtn_addr and branch here (vector 4), to
    take_fline (vector 11) or take_aline (vector 10).  The handler is
    normally take_exception, but can be replaced.
0D754 vector offset 0x10: illegal instruction
@0D760 take_fline
0D764 vector offset 0x2C: line 1111 emulator
@0D770 take_aline
0D778 vector offset 0x28: line 1010 emulator

@0D780 take_exception
    ===========================================================================
    Exception entry
    ===========================================================================
    Build a 68K exception stack frame and continue at the handler named by
    the vector.

    On entry: immed_data = format/vector word for the frame (format in bits
    15:12, vector offset in bits 11:0).  Entry at take_exception_sr_built is
    used by callers that have already put the SR high byte in data and set
    or cleared cr7.so themselves; cr7.so set means the caller wants control
    back (through LR) after the stack has been switched, so that it can push
    the extra words of a longer frame before the common SR/PC/format words.
0D780 plain frame: no extra words
0D784 data = SR high byte, in place
@0D788 take_exception_sr_built
0D788 pc = address of the instruction
0D78C clear T1, T0 and trace_pending
@0D790 take_exception_pc_set
0D790 CCR bit 4 (X) from ccr_x
0D798 CCR bit 3 (N) from cr0.lt
0D79C CCR bit 2 (Z) from cr0.eq
0D7A4 CCR bits 1:0 (V, C) from XER[OV], XER[CA]
@0D7A8 enter_supervisor
0D7A8 already in supervisor state
0D7B0 save the user stack pointer
0D7B4 assume the interrupt stack
0D7B8 set SR S bit
0D7C4 M bit set: the master stack instead
@0D7C8 push_exception_frame
0D7C8 let the caller push its extra frame words first
0D7CC push SR
0D7D0 push PC
0D7D4 vector offset
0D7D8 pc = handler address from the vector table
0D7DC push format/vector word
0D7E4 cr7.so = low bit of the new pc
0D7F4 odd handler address: address error
0D7F8 clear flag_group_1_active

@0E5BC
    ===========================================================================
    Floating point instructions
    ===========================================================================
    A 68LC040 has no floating point unit.  It takes a line-F exception for an
    FPU opcode, but with a frame of format 4, which adds the effective
    address of the instruction's operand and the address of the instruction
    itself so that a software FPU need not decode the addressing mode.  The
    emulator does the same.
    .
    Each kind of FPU opcode and addressing mode has a routine here that
    loads one of forty handler slots (+0x89C to +0x938) and goes to
    take_fline.  The slots start out as take_exception, which makes an
    ordinary format 0 frame; install_exception_stubs fills them with the
    routines at fpu_stubs, which make the format 4 frame.

@0E6CC rte_format_4
    RTE from a format 4 frame (installed in the RTE handler table by
    install_exception_stubs): discard the two extra long words and carry
    on as for format 0.

@0E6D4 fpu_stubs
    The handlers for the forty slots.  Each works out the operand's
    effective address in addr (0 when there is none) and the instruction's
    address in rtn_addr, and moves pc past the instruction's extension
    words, according to the kind of opcode and addressing mode its slot
    stands for; the last four groups are the privileged FSAVE and FRESTORE.

@0E794 fpu_format_4_exception
    Take the line-F exception with a format 4 frame.  take_exception_pc_set
    comes back here for the two extra long words before it pushes the rest.
0E79C format 4, vector offset 0x2C
0E7A8 the effective address ...
0E7AC ... and the address of the instruction

